From e5eeea41bff52f03e1e9c9a44b9d401d51911b88 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ochafik <olivier.chafik@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 3 Jun 2026 00:42:18 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] wasm2js: flatten deeply-nested block/if chains to bound JS
 nesting depth

Toolchains like LLVM routinely emit functions whose structured control
flow is a very deep tower of nested wasm `block` and `if` constructs
(thousands of levels deep for large functions, switches lowered via
`br_table`, and emscripten's JS-based exception / longjmp lowering).
wasm2js naively turns each named block into a JS labeled block
`label : { ... }` and each `if` into a JS `if (c) { ... }`, so such a
tower becomes deeply-nested JS that JS-engine parsers recurse over and
overflow at parse time (`RangeError: Maximum call stack size
exceeded`) before the code ever runs. Reproduced on a real OpenSCAD
wasm2js build: max JS nesting depth ~1500, which neither Node nor a
browser Worker could compile.

This makes wasm2js track the JS nesting depth it is emitting into and,
once that crosses a small constant threshold (40), lower the remaining
chain of nested named blocks / then-only ifs into a single shallow
`while (1) { switch (state) { ... } }` dispatch loop instead. Each
`Block` link contributes a prefix segment (code before the chain
child) and a tail segment (code after it); each `If` link contributes
a guard segment that jumps past the body when the condition is false.
Switch fall-through models normal control flow (entering the next
link; falling out of an innermost body into each enclosing link's
tail). A `br` to a flattened block becomes `state = <segment>;
continue $L`, which works from any JS nesting depth. The generated JS
depth is thus bounded by a small constant regardless of input size;
ordinary shallow code stays below the threshold and is emitted
unchanged, so existing test expectations are untouched.

The regression test (test/wasm2js/deep-control-flow.wast) covers pure
block towers with fall-through, branches to enclosing blocks via
br_if, a large br_table, deep if-chains, mixed block/if interleaving,
sibling chains (unique dispatch-label generation), a side-effecting
condition evaluated exactly once through the dispatch, nested dispatch
loops (a chain interrupted by a loop holding another deep chain), and
a counting loop coexisting with a flattened chain. 54 assert_return
cases verify execution equivalence; expected values were checked
against the Binaryen interpreter (wasm-shell) as ground truth.

Verified end-to-end on the real openscad.wasm: max output nesting
depth 428 -> 70, `node --check` passes at the default stack.

